                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><span style="font-family: 'times new roman', times, serif;">Systematic Anatomy, one of the oldest and most classic basic medical courses, is a required course for medical students offered in the fall semester. The course explains the morphology, functions, and developmental patterns of normal human organs according to organ functional systems (such as musculoskeletal, digestive, nervous, etc.), serving as a core discipline in basic medicine. Its goal is to help students master the morphology, positions, and interrelationships of organs, laying a solid morphological foundation for subsequent medical studies. Knowledge of anatomy is essential for clinical diagnosis, treatment, surgery, public disease prevention, health promotion.</span></p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
